{"type":"after","callId":"call@430","endTime":53523.43,"result":{"value":{"s":"\n    Home/Manual/Developer reference/The IR word — what a shot carries through the air\n    \n\nThe IR word — what a shot carries through the air\nA 25-bit pulse-width-encoded word on a 38 kHz carrier, decoded from LaserTagMods' base-station source and verified on our own receiver and emitter\n✅benchLast verified 2026-08-27View as Markdown\nWhat the badges mean✅bench Verified on our bench📖official Official Battle Company docs🔍app Decoded from the Callsign app👥community Community-reported🧪software Built + software-tested📐spec Specified only🚧under construction Under constructionNothing unconfirmed is published — see Credits & sourcing.\n\nCredit.The layout was decoded from LaserTagMods' NRFL-Bases  (a referee-free domination base that receives BRX shots), then bench-verified: timings, bit count, field offsets and the parity rule were all confirmed by pushing known  frames over BLE and watching only the expected bits move. A stock tagger then accepted a fully synthetic word from our emitter (invented player 42 / team 2 / damage 33 landed as a real  and killed the player).Source: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md\nPhysical layerCarrier 38 kHz, 940/980 nm — a standard VS1838B/TSOP demodulating receiver recovers it. Laser rated 16.9 mW on the gun's USB record.Sync:one ~2 ms LOW pulse before the frame — measured 1988–1991 µs.\nBits:each bit is a LOW pulse; long ≈ 1000 µs = 1 (measured 990–994), short ≈ 500 µs = 0 (489–512), spaces 489–512 µs; decision threshold ~750 µs.\nEnd of frame:a trailing short pulse (< 250 µs in node1's test).\n⚠ A  sync gate is not BRX-unique (a Sony SIRC remote's 2390 µs header passes it). Bound sync to ~1800–2200 µs and require 25 bits + the parity rule.Source: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md; protocol/brx-protocol.md §7c (laser mW)\nWord layout (transmit order after sync; 25 bits)\n\n\nField\nBits\nOffset\nMeaning\nBench evidence\nConf\n\n\n\nB\n4\n0–3\nIR protocol / damage type =  t3 =  tok2 =  protocol key\nAR read 0; rocket (t3=10) read 10; native melee read 13\n✅bench\n\n\nP\n6\n4–9\nplayer id 0–63 =  token 1 =  tok3\nmatched the registry\n✅bench\n\n\nT\n2\n10–11\nteam id 0–3 =  =  tok4\nmatched\n✅bench\n\n\nD\n8\n12–19\nmagnitude =  t5 =  tok5\npushed 22 → 9 → 115; only these bits moved\n✅bench\n\n\nC\n1\n20\ncritical flag →  tok6, ×1.5 applied\nemitted crit=1 → \n✅bench\n\n\nU\n2\n21–22\n subtype →  tok7\nU=0/1/3 registered with rows; U=2 (no row) ignored\n✅bench\n\n\nZ\n2\n23–24\nparity trailer\nsee rule\n✅bench\n\n\nSource: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md; docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-26 melee capture)\nDEV-06diagram pendingBit-field ruler of the 25-bit word with the sync pulse and a sample pulse train ( = protocol 13, player 7, team 1, magnitude 90, crit 0, subtype 1 — a genuine captured melee swing).Source: docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-26 melee)\nParity — what genuine frames emit vs what the gun checks.Real BRX frames set Z by parity over bits 0–22: odd number of ones → , even →  (4/4 captured frames). But the gun's acceptance test is only :  and  both land 8/8,  and  are rejected 0/8. Compute the true parity for fidelity; use the mismatch to tell your own traffic from a real gun's.Source: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md\n\nSource: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md (rule); illustrative implementation\nNative emissions captured off the air\n\n\nSource\nWord\nNote\nConf\n\n\n\nAssault Rifle\nproto 0, magnitude 9\nThe stock AR emits 9, not the manual's 24\n✅bench\n\n\nShotgun\nproto 0, magnitude 45\n\n✅bench\n\n\nSniper\nproto 0, subtype 1, magnitude 80\n\n✅bench\n\n\nRocket Launcher\nproto 10, magnitude 115\n\n✅bench\n\n\nMelee (gyro swing, native game)\nproto 13, subtype 1, magnitude 90\nSubtype 1 = Rifle Bash\n✅bench\n\n\nSupremacy Sentinel death-nova (headset)\nproto 10, magnitude 125, player/team = the dying player\nOut-damages the rocket; credits kills to the corpse\n✅bench\n\n\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §7r addendum; docs/experiment-log.md\nHeadset emission cannot be forced over BLE., ,  and  produced zero IR with a receiver control passing before and after. The headset emits only for a physical melee swing in a native game and for the Sentinel death-nova.Source: docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-26 headset emission)\nDo I need a victim gun to test an emitter?No — a VS1838B on an ESP32 decodes the word, and the sync/mark timings above are the acceptance spec.\nCan a station revive a dead player by IR?No. A dead gun ignores all IR; stations arm a living tagger's respawn path.\nWhy do my captured frames come out as prefixes (16/17/20/21 bits)?Your capture sketch is printing while the next frame lands. Turn the RAW dump off.\nSource: protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md · docs/experiment-log.md (448-word brute force) · docs/gotchas.md; protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md (capture gotcha)\n\n\n  "}},"afterSnapshot":"after@call@430"}
